['Here is a basic implementation in C using POSIX threads (pthreads) for semaphores. This code does not include logging mechanism but it provides a good starting point for your requirement. 

```c
#include <stdio.h>
#include <stdlib.h>
#include <pthread.h>
#include <semaphore.h>

#define MAX_SEMAPHORES 500

typedef struct {
    sem_t *semaphores;
    int count;
} SemaphoreManager;

SemaphoreManager* createSemaphoreManager(int initialValues[], int count) {
    if (count > MAX_SEMAPHORES || count < 1) {
        printf("Invalid semaphore count\n");
        return NULL;
    }

    SemaphoreManager *manager = malloc(sizeof(SemaphoreManager));
    manager->semaphores = malloc(count * sizeof(sem_t));
    manager->count = count;

    for (int i = 0; i < count; ++i) {
        if (sem_init(&manager->semaphores[i], 0, initialValues[i]) == -1) {
            printf("Failed to initialize semaphore\n");
            return NULL;
        }
    }

    return manager;
}

void destroySemaphoreManager(SemaphoreManager *manager) {
    for (int i = 0; i < manager->count; ++i) {
        sem_destroy(&manager->semaphores[i]);
    }

    free(manager->semaphores);
    free(manager);
}

void waitSemaphore(SemaphoreManager *manager, int index) {
    if (index < 0 || index >= manager->count) {
        printf("Invalid semaphore index\n");
        return;
    }

    sem_wait(&manager->semaphores[index]);
}

void signalSemaphore(SemaphoreManager *manager, int index) {
    if (index < 0 || index >= manager->count) {
        printf("Invalid semaphore index\n");
        return;
    }

    sem_post(&manager->semaphores[index]);
}
```
This code creates a `SemaphoreManager` struct that holds an array of semaphores and their count. The `createSemaphoreManager` function initializes the manager with the given initial values, while `destroySemaphoreManager` frees up memory when it's no longer needed. 

The `waitSemaphore` and `signalSemaphore` functions are used to wait and signal semaphores respectively. These functions handle invalid index errors by printing an error message and returning early.

This code does not include a logging mechanism, but you could easily add one by adding print statements or writing to a file at key points in your program. 

Please note that this is a basic implementation and may need adjustments based on the specific requirements of your project. For example, if you expect many threads to be waiting on semaphores concurrently, you might want to consider using a condition variable instead of a semaphore for better performance. Similarly, if you anticipate needing to dynamically add or remove semaphores, you'll need to modify the `createSemaphoreManager` function to handle this.
']

