['Here's a basic implementation of your requirement using Java 8 Stream API with custom exception handling. This code doesn't include thread pool management or memory optimization as it would require more specific details about the system environment, but it should give you a good starting point.

```java
import java.security.MessageDigest;
import java.security.NoSuchAlgorithmException;
import java.util.stream.Stream;

public class MD5HashUtility {
    private static final int MAX_LENGTH = 4096;

    public String process(Stream<String> input) throws NoSuchAlgorithmException, MaxLengthExceededException {
        MessageDigest md = MessageDigest.getInstance("MD5");

        for (String s : input.toArray(String[]::new)) {
            if (s.length() > MAX_LENGTH) {
                throw new MaxLengthExceededException();
            }

            md.update(s.getBytes());
        }

        byte[] digest = md.digest();
        StringBuilder sb = new StringBuilder();
        for (byte b : digest) {
            sb.append(String.format("%02x", b & 0xff));
        }

        return sb.toString();
    }

    public static class MaxLengthExceededException extends Exception {
        public MaxLengthExceededException() {
            super("Input string length exceeded the maximum limit of " + MAX_LENGTH);
        }
    }
}
```

This utility class processes a stream of strings, validating each one's length and throwing an exception if it exceeds the allowed limit. It then computes the MD5 hash of all inputs concatenated together. 

Please note that this code is not thread-safe and doesn't handle concurrent access to shared resources or large volumes of data efficiently. For a production environment, you would need to add appropriate synchronization mechanisms (like locks or atomic variables) and consider using a thread pool for better performance under heavy load conditions. 

Also, the code assumes that all strings are processed at once, which might not be feasible in a real-world scenario where data is streamed continuously. In such cases, you would need to modify the process method to handle each string individually and update the hash incrementally.
']

