["Here is an example of how you might implement such a function in JavaScript using Node.js with async/await for asynchronous processing, streams for handling large data feeds, and zlib for decompression. This code does not include error handling or logging, which would be necessary for production-level code. 

```javascript
const { createHash } = require('crypto');
const { pipeline } = require('stream/promises');
const { createGunzip } = require('zlib');
const fs = require('fs').promises;

class DataProcessor {
    constructor() {
        this.buffer = Buffer.alloc(0);
        this.checksum = createHash('sha256');
    }

    async processData(data) {
        // Append incoming data to buffer
        this.buffer = Buffer.concat([this.buffer, data]);

        while (true) {
            if (this.buffer.length < 4) break;  // need at least 4 bytes for length field

            const length = this.buffer.readUInt32BE(0);
            if (this.buffer.length < length + 4) break;  // not enough data yet

            const chunk = this.buffer.slice(4, length + 4);
            this.buffer = this.buffer.slice(length + 4);

            await pipeline(chunk, createGunzip(), fs.writeFile('./data', { flag: 'a' }));  // append to file
        }
    }

    async computeChecksum() {
        const hash = this.checksum;
        return new Promise((resolve, reject) => {
            pipeline(fs.createReadStream('./data'), hash).then(() => resolve(hash.read())).catch(reject);
        });
    }
}
```
This code assumes that the data feed is a series of gzip-compressed chunks prefixed with their length as a big-endian 4-byte integer. It buffers incoming data until it has enough to extract a complete chunk, then decompresses and writes it to a file. The checksum is computed by reading from the file while it's being written.

Please note that this code does not handle errors or edge cases such as network interruptions, concurrent processing, synchronization, logging, etc. These would need to be added for production-level code. Also, the storage format used here (raw files on disk) is quite simple and may not meet all requirements in a real-world scenario.
