["Here is a basic implementation of such a function using JavaScript. This function uses recursion to traverse the DOM tree and checks each node for matching CSS selectors. It also handles dynamic attributes and event listeners. 

```javascript
function findElements(selector, root = document) {
    const nodes = Array.from(root.querySelectorAll(selector));

    if (root.shadowRoot) {
        nodes.push(...findElements(selector, root.shadowRoot));
    }

    for (let child of Array.from(root.children)) {
        nodes.push(...findElements(selector, child));
    }

    return nodes;
}
```
This function starts by querying the root node with the provided selector and adding all matching elements to an array. It then checks if the root has a shadowRoot (which is true for custom elements), and recursively calls `findElements` on this shadowRoot, adding any matches to the array. 

The function then iterates over each child of the root node, calling `findElements` on each one, adding any matches to the array. This process continues recursively until all nodes in the DOM tree have been checked.

