['Here is an example of how you might implement such a client in Rust using the `reqwest` crate for HTTP requests and `tracing` for logging and monitoring. This code does not include all the features mentioned in your requirements due to space limitations but it should give you a good starting point. 

```rust
use reqwest::Client;
use tracing::info;
use std::sync::Arc;
use hyper::header::Headers;

pub struct HttpClient {
    client: Arc<Client>,
}

impl HttpClient {
    pub fn new() -> Self {
        let client = Client::builder().build().unwrap();
        Self {
            client: Arc::new(client),
        }
    }

    pub async fn get(&self, url: &str) -> Result<String, reqwest::Error> {
        info_span::info_span("http_request", |span| {
            span.record("url", &url);
            let res = self.client.get(url).send().await?;
            let body = res.text().await?;
            Ok(body)
        })
    }

    pub async fn post(&self, url: &str, headers: Option<Headers>, body: Option<&str>) -> Result<String, reqwest::Error> {
        info_span::info_span("http_request", |span| {
            span.record("url", &url);
            let mut request = self.client.post(url);
            if let Some(headers) = headers {
                request = request.headers(headers);
            }
            if let Some(body) = body {
                request = request.body(body);
            }
            let res = request.send().await?;
            let body = res.text().await?;
            Ok(body)
        })
    }
}
```
This code creates a `HttpClient` struct that wraps a `reqwest::Client` instance and provides methods for making GET and POST requests. It uses the `info_span` macro from the `tracing` crate to log information about each HTTP request, which can be integrated with an external logging system like Prometheus or Jaeger.

Please note that this is a simplified example and does not cover all of your requirements. For instance, it doesn't handle certificate validation, custom headers, body data, dynamic dependency management, performance constraints, integration issues, scalability, usability, resource management etc. You would need to add these features as per your specific needs.
