['Here's a basic implementation in C using POSIX threads (pthreads) for managing tasks. This code does not include error handling or logging, but it should give you an idea of how to structure your function. 

```c
#include <stdio.h>
#include <stdlib.h>
#include <unistd.h>
#include <pthread.h>

#define MAX_TASKS 10
#define MAX_CONCURRENT_TASKS 5

typedef struct {
    char *command;
} Task;

void* executeTask(void *arg) {
    Task *task = (Task*) arg;

    // Execute task here. For simplicity, we'll just sleep for a second.
    printf("Executing task: %s\n", task->command);
    sleep(1);  // Simulate work being done.

    free(task->command);
    free(task);

    return NULL;
}

void manageTasks(Task *tasks, int num_tasks) {
    pthread_t threads[MAX_CONCURRENT_TASKS];
    int thread_count = 0;
    int task_index = 0;

    while (task_index < num_tasks) {
        // Start a new task if we have room.
        if (thread_count < MAX_CONCURRENT_TASKS) {
            pthread_create(&threads[thread_count], NULL, executeTask, tasks + task_index);
            ++task_index;
            ++thread_count;
        } else {
            // Wait for a thread to finish.
            --thread_count;
            pthread_join(threads[thread_count], NULL);
        }
    }

    // Wait for all threads to finish.
    while (thread_count > 0) {
        --thread_count;
        pthread_join(threads[thread_count], NULL);
    }
}
```
This code uses a simple round-robin scheduling algorithm, starting new tasks as old ones complete. It also properly frees resources when they're no longer needed. However, it doesn't handle errors or signals, and it doesn't implement any kind of resource management to prevent exhaustion under high load conditions.

For a more robust solution, you would need to add error handling, signal handling, and possibly some form of resource limiting (e.g., using ulimit on Unix-like systems). You might also want to consider using a library like libdispatch or Boost.Asio for managing tasks and resources, depending on your platform and needs.
']

