['Here is a basic implementation of the problem in Java. This code assumes you have a class `Schema` which maps byte offsets to field values and a class `DeserializationException` that extends `RuntimeException` for handling deserialization failures. 

```java
import java.nio.ByteBuffer;
import java.util.concurrent.locks.ReentrantLock;

public class Deserializer {
    private final ReentrantLock lock = new ReentrantLock();

    public Object deserialize(byte[] data, Schema schema) throws DeserializationException {
        lock.lock();

        try {
            if (schema == null) {
                throw new DeserializationException("Schema is not available at runtime");
            }

            ByteBuffer buffer = ByteBuffer.wrap(data);
            Object obj = schema.getObjectType().newInstance();

            for (Field field : schema.getFields()) {
                int offset = field.getOffset();

                if (buffer.remaining() < offset) {
                    throw new DeserializationException("Unexpected byte sequence");
                }

                buffer.position(offset);

                switch (field.getType()) {
                    case BYTE:
                        field.setByte(obj, buffer.get());
                        break;
                    case SHORT:
                        field.setShort(obj, buffer.getShort());
                        break;
                    case INT:
                        field.setInt(obj, buffer.getInt());
                        break;
                    case LONG:
                        field.setLong(obj, buffer.getLong());
                        break;
                    case FLOAT:
                        field.setFloat(obj, buffer.getFloat());
                        break;
                    case DOUBLE:
                        field.setDouble(obj, buffer.getDouble());
                        break;
                    default:
                        throw new DeserializationException("Unsupported type");
                }
            }

            return obj;
        } catch (IllegalAccessException | InstantiationException e) {
            throw new DeserializationException(e);
        } finally {
            lock.unlock();
        }
    }
}
```
This code uses a `ReentrantLock` to ensure thread-safe access to shared resources, such as the schema and the byte buffer. It also handles potential deserialization failures by throwing a `DeserializationException` with a detailed error message. The method efficiently manages memory and optimizes deserialization speed while minimizing object creation and copying.
']

