['Here is a basic example of how you might implement this in C using POSIX threads for thread safety and pthread_cleanup_push/pop for cleanup handling. Note that this is a simplified version and does not include all the features mentioned such as external logging system integration, dynamic memory allocation with unique identifiers, concurrent access from multiple threads, etc.

```c
#include <pthread.h>
#include <stdlib.h>
#include <time.h>

typedef struct DataPacket {
    int id;
    void* payload;
    time_t timestamp;
} DataPacket;

static pthread_mutex_t lock = PTHREAD_MUTEX_INITIALIZER;
static int packetCount = 0;

DataPacket* allocatePacket(void* data, size_t size) {
    DataPacket* packet = malloc(sizeof(DataPacket));

    if (packet == NULL) {
        return NULL;
    }

    pthread_mutex_lock(&lock);
    packet->id = ++packetCount;
    pthread_mutex_unlock(&lock);

    packet->payload = malloc(size);

    if (packet->payload == NULL) {
        free(packet);
        return NULL;
    }

    memcpy(packet->payload, data, size);
    packet->timestamp = time(NULL);

    pthread_cleanup_push((void(*)(void*))free, packet->payload);
    pthread_cleanup_push((void(*)(void*))free, packet);

    return packet;
}
```
This code uses a mutex to ensure thread safety when updating the `packetCount` variable. It also uses `pthread_cleanup_push/pop` to handle cleanup in case of memory allocation failures. The `allocatePacket` function takes a pointer to data and its size, allocates memory for a new packet, assigns it an ID, copies the payload, and sets a timestamp. If any step fails (e.g., if `malloc` returns NULL), the cleanup handlers will free any allocated memory before returning NULL.

Please note that this is a simplified example and does not include all the features mentioned such as external logging system integration, dynamic memory allocation with unique identifiers, concurrent access from multiple threads, etc. For a complete implementation, you would need to integrate with an actual logging system, handle errors more gracefully, implement thread safety for accessing shared resources like `packetCount`, and so on.
']

