['Here is a basic implementation of such a function using C programming language. This code does not include all the features you requested but it provides a starting point for your requirements. 

```c
#include <stdio.h>
#include <stdlib.h>
#include <pthread.h>
#define MAX_CONNECTIONS 1000

typedef struct {
    void *memory;
    size_t size;
} AllocationRecord;

AllocationRecord memoryPool[MAX_CONNECTIONS];
int currentAllocations = 0;
pthread_mutex_t mutex = PTHREAD_MUTEX_INITIALIZER;

void *allocateMemory(size_t size) {
    pthread_mutex_lock(&mutex);

    if (currentAllocations >= MAX_CONNECTIONS) {
        printf("Failed to allocate memory. Max connections reached.\n");
        return NULL;
    }

    void *memory = malloc(size);
    if (!memory) {
        printf("Failed to allocate memory.\n");
        pthread_mutex_unlock(&mutex);
        return NULL;
    }

    AllocationRecord record = { .memory = memory, .size = size };
    memoryPool[currentAllocations++] = record;

    printf("Successfully allocated %zu bytes of memory.\n", size);
    pthread_mutex_unlock(&mutex);

    return memory;
}

void freeMemory(void *memory) {
    pthread_mutex_lock(&mutex);

    for (int i = 0; i < currentAllocations; ++i) {
        if (memoryPool[i].memory == memory) {
            free(memory);

            // Shift all elements after the freed one to fill the gap.
            for (int j = i; j < currentAllocations - 1; ++j) {
                memoryPool[j] = memoryPool[j + 1];
            }

            --currentAllocations;
            printf("Successfully freed allocated memory.\n");
            pthread_mutex_unlock(&mutex);
            return;
        }
    }

    printf("Failed to free memory. Memory not found in pool.\n");
    pthread_mutex_unlock(&mutex);
}
```
This code uses a simple array as a memory pool and keeps track of the current allocations using a counter. It also uses a mutex for thread safety, which ensures that only one thread can access the memory pool at a time. 

Please note that this is a very basic implementation and does not include many features you requested such as concurrent requests handling, connection limits tracking, robust logging system etc. You would need to add these features according to your specific requirements. Also, please remember that C is not the best language for high-traffic web services due to its lack of built-in support for concurrency and memory safety. For such applications, you might want to consider using a more suitable language like Rust or Go.
