The choice of synchronization strategy is based on the assumption that most of the time, there will be no contention for the lock (i.e., the function will not be called concurrently by multiple threads). If this assumption holds true, a simple mutex should provide adequate performance and simplicity. However, if there's a high degree of contention or if the function is expected to be called concurrently frequently, more complex synchronization strategies such as reader-writer locks or fine-grained locking may be necessary.
