['Here is a basic example of how you might implement this in C using pthreads for thread safety and error handling. This code does not include logging or memory management optimizations which would be necessary to handle large amounts of data efficiently, but it should give you a good starting point. 

```c
#include <pthread.h>
#include <stdlib.h>
#include <string.h>

typedef struct {
    int sequence_number;
    long timestamp;
    int priority_level;
} TSN_PACKET_INFO_T;

typedef struct Node {
    TSN_PACKET_INFO_T data;
    struct Node* next;
} Node;

pthread_mutex_t lock = PTHREAD_MUTEX_INITIALIZER;
Node *head = NULL;
int count = 0;
int maxSize;

void addElement(TSN_PACKET_INFO_T data) {
    pthread_mutex_lock(&lock);

    if (count >= maxSize) {
        printf("Error: List is full\n");
        return;
    }

    Node *newNode = malloc(sizeof(Node));
    if (newNode == NULL) {
        printf("Memory allocation failed\n");
        pthread_mutex_unlock(&lock);
        return;
    }

    newNode->data = data;
    newNode->next = head;
    head = newNode;
    count++;

    printf("Added element with sequence number %d\n", data.sequence_number);

    pthread_mutex_unlock(&lock);
}

void clearList() {
    pthread_mutex_lock(&lock);

    Node *current = head;
    while (current != NULL) {
        Node *temp = current;
        current = current->next;
        free(temp);
    }

    count = 0;
    head = NULL;

    pthread_mutex_unlock(&lock);
}
```
This code uses a linked list to store the elements. It also uses a mutex for thread safety, ensuring that only one thread can access the list at a time. The `addElement` function checks if the list is full before adding an element and handles memory allocation failures by printing an error message. The `clearList` function frees all allocated nodes when called.

Please note that this code does not include logging or memory management optimizations which would be necessary to handle large amounts of data efficiently, but it should give you a good starting point. Also, the design and implementation of such a system can have significant impacts on the overall system architecture and integration with external components, so thorough testing and performance analysis are crucial for ensuring that this code meets your specific requirements.
