["Here's a basic implementation of a thread-safe linked list in C using atomic operations. This code does not include resizing or clearing the list, as those would require more complex implementations that are beyond the scope of this task. 

```c
#include <stdatomic.h>
#include <stdlib.h>

typedef struct NFAPI_CRC_INDICATION_PDU_T {
    int RX_UE_INFORMATION_TAG;
    int RNTI;
    atomic_int CRC_INDICATION_REL8_TAG;
} NFAPI_CRC_INDICATION_PDU_T;

typedef struct Node {
    NFAPI_CRC_INDICATION_PDU_T data;
    struct Node* next;
} Node;

atomic_int count = ATOMIC_VAR_INIT(0);
Node* head = NULL;

void insert(NFAPI_CRC_INDICATION_PDU_T value) {
    Node* newNode = malloc(sizeof(Node));
    newNode->data = value;
    newNode->next = atomic_load(&head);

    while(!atomic_compare_exchange_weak(&head, &newNode->next, newNode)) {}

    atomic_fetch_add(&count, 1);
}

void delete(int RNTI) {
    Node* curr = head;
    Node* prev = NULL;

    while (curr != NULL && curr->data.RNTI != RNTI) {
        prev = curr;
        curr = curr->next;
    }

    if (curr == NULL) return; // value not found

    if (prev == NULL) {
        head = curr->next;
    } else {
        prev->next = curr->next;
    }

    free(curr);
    atomic_fetch_sub(&count, 1);
}
```
This code uses the C11 `<stdatomic.h>` library to provide atomic operations for thread safety. The linked list is implemented as a singly-linked list with a head pointer that points to the first node in the list. Each node contains an NFAPI_CRC_INDICATION_PDU_T structure and a pointer to the next node.

The `insert` function creates a new node, sets its data field to the provided value, and inserts it at the head of the list using atomic compare-and-swap operations. This ensures that multiple threads can safely insert nodes into the list without causing race conditions or memory leaks.

The `delete` function searches for a node with a matching RNTI in the list and removes it if found. It also updates the count of nodes in the list using atomic fetch-add and fetch-sub operations to ensure thread safety.

Please note that this is a basic implementation and does not include resizing or clearing the list, as those would require more complex implementations that are beyond the scope of this task. Also, it's important to remember that C doesn't have built-in support for garbage collection, so you need to manually free any memory allocated with `malloc` when it's no longer needed.
"]

