['Here's a basic implementation of the problem in Java using Apache Commons Codec library to compute MD5 hash. This solution assumes that the input stream can be processed asynchronously for better performance. It also uses ConcurrentHashMap for thread safety. 

```java
import org.apache.commons.codec.digest.DigestUtils;
import java.util.concurrent.*;
import java.util.stream.*;
import java.security.*;
import java.util.*;

public class StringHasher {
    private ConcurrentHashMap<String, String> cache = new ConcurrentHashMap<>();

    public Map<String, String> processStream(Stream<String> stream) throws NoSuchAlgorithmException {
        ExecutorService executor = Executors.newFixedThreadPool(10); // adjust based on your needs

        stream.forEach(s -> {
            if (s == null || s.isEmpty()) {
                System.out.println("Warning: Null or empty string detected.");
                return;
            }

            executor.submit(() -> {
                try {
                    String hash = DigestUtils.md5Hex(s);
                    cache.put(s, hash);
                } catch (Exception e) {
                    System.out.println("Error processing string: " + s);
                    e.printStackTrace();
                }
            });
        });

        executor.shutdown();
        try {
            if (!executor.awaitTermination(60, TimeUnit.SECONDS)) {
                executor.shutdownNow();
            }
        } catch (InterruptedException ex) {
            executor.shutdownNow();
            Thread.currentThread().interrupt();
        }

        return cache;
    }
}
```
This code creates a thread pool of 10 threads to process the stream asynchronously, and uses ConcurrentHashMap for thread safety. It also handles exceptions during hashing by printing an error message and stack trace. The method returns when all tasks have completed or after a timeout of 60 seconds.
']

