['Here is a simple implementation in C that meets all the requirements you specified. This code uses a dynamic array to store entries, which allows for efficient memory usage and iteration speed. The resizing strategy is quite basic - it doubles the capacity each time it needs to resize. 

```c
#include <stdio.h>
#include <stdlib.h>
#include <string.h>
#include <time.h>

typedef struct {
    char* key;
    time_t timestamp;
    double value;
} Entry;

typedef struct {
    Entry* entries;
    size_t capacity;
    size_t count;
} Collection;

void initCollection(Collection* collection) {
    collection->capacity = 10;
    collection->count = 0;
    collection->entries = malloc(sizeof(Entry) * collection->capacity);
}

void freeCollection(Collection* collection) {
    for (int i = 0; i < collection->count; ++i) {
        free(collection->entries[i].key);
    }
    free(collection->entries);
}

void addEntry(Collection* collection, const char* key, time_t timestamp, double value) {
    if (collection->count == collection->capacity) {
        collection->capacity *= 2;
        collection->entries = realloc(collection->entries, sizeof(Entry) * collection->capacity);
    }

    size_t keyLength = strlen(key);
    collection->entries[collection->count].key = malloc(keyLength + 1);
    strncpy(collection->entries[collection->count].key, key, keyLength);
    collection->entries[collection->count].timestamp = timestamp;
    collection->entries[collection->count].value = value;
    ++collection->count;
}

void printEntries(Collection* collection) {
    for (int i = 0; i < collection->count; ++i) {
        printf("Key: %s, Timestamp: %ld, Value: %f\n", 
               collection->entries[i].key, 
               collection->entries[i].timestamp, 
               collection->entries[i].value);
    }
}
```
This code is quite simple and efficient. However, it does not handle memory fragmentation issues very well. If you add a lot of entries and then remove them, the memory will still be allocated but unused. This could lead to performance problems if you need to allocate large amounts of memory in the future. 

Also, this code uses a simple resizing strategy that doubles the capacity each time it needs to resize. This is efficient for most cases, but can lead to significant overhead when the collection grows very quickly. A more complex resizing strategy could be used to reduce the number of times we need to resize, at the cost of increased complexity and potential performance degradation in other scenarios.
']

