['This is a complex task that requires a deep understanding of Rust, concurrency models, and build systems. Here's an outline of how you might approach this problem in Rust. This code doesn't cover all your requirements but it gives you a starting point to implement them. 

```rust
use std::time::Instant;
use std::sync::{Arc, Mutex};
use std::thread;
use std::collections::HashMap;
use std::process::Command;
use std::net::TcpStream;
use serde_json::Value as JsonValue;

struct BuildCache {
    cache: Arc<Mutex<HashMap<String, String>>>,
}

impl BuildCache {
    fn new() -> Self {
        BuildCache {
            cache: Arc::new(Mutex::new(HashMap::new())),
        }
    }

    fn get(&self, key: &str) -> Option<&String> {
        self.cache.lock().unwrap().get(key)
    }

    fn insert(&self, key: String, value: String) {
        self.cache.lock().unwrap().insert(key, value);
    }
}

struct BuildManager {
    cache: BuildCache,
    analytics_service: TcpStream,
}

impl BuildManager {
    fn new() -> Self {
        let socket_addr = "127.0.0.1:8080".parse().unwrap();
        let analytics_service = TcpStream::connect(socket_addr).unwrap();

        BuildManager {
            cache: BuildCache::new(),
            analytics_service,
        }
    }

    fn build(&self) -> Result<String, String> {
        let start = Instant::now();

        // Compile dependencies and execute tests
        let output = Command::new("cargo")
            .arg("test")
            .output()
            .map_err(|e| format_err("Failed to compile", e))?;

        if !output.status.success() {
            return Err(format_err("Compilation failed", output));
        }

        let end = Instant::now();
        let duration = end - start;

        // Report build time to analytics service
        thread::spawn(move || {
            let mut json = JsonValue::object();
            json.insert("build_time".to_string(), JsonValue::Number((duration.as_secs() as f64) + (duration.subsec_nanos() as f64 / 1e9)));
            let serialized = serde_json::to_vec(&json).unwrap();
            self.analytics_service.write_all(&serialized).unwrap();
        });

        // Return build output
        Ok(String::from_utf8(output.stdout)?)
    }
}
```
This code creates a `BuildManager` struct that manages the build process and reports the build time to an analytics service over TCP. It uses a simple in-memory cache for storing compiled artifacts, which can be extended to use disk storage or a distributed cache like Redis. 

Please note that this is just a starting point and doesn't cover all your requirements. You would need to add error handling, logging, concurrency control, build optimization, integration with external systems, etc. Also, please remember that Rust has its own set of challenges when it comes to writing complex multi-threaded code, so you might need to use more advanced techniques or libraries for some parts of this task.
']

