['Here is a basic implementation of the module in JavaScript using Neo4j as graph database API. This example does not cover all tradeoffs you mentioned but it provides a good starting point for your requirements. 

```javascript
const neo4j = require('neo4j-driver');
const randomstring = require("randomstring");

class GraphManager {
    constructor(uri, user, password) {
        this.driver = neo4j.driver(uri, neo4j.auth.basic(user, password));
    }

    async addVertex(label, properties) {
        const session = this.driver.session();
        try {
            await session.run(`CREATE (:${label} ${properties})`);
        } finally {
            await session.close();
        }
    }

    async addEdge(from, to) {
        const session = this.driver.session();
        try {
            await session.run(`MATCH (a:${from}), (b:${to}) CREATE (a)-[:RELATION]->(b)`);
        } finally {
            await session.close();
        }
    }
}

class DomTraverser {
    constructor() {}

    traverseDom(root, selector) {
        const stack = [root];
        const result = [];

        while (stack.length) {
            let node = stack.pop();

            if (node.matches(selector)) {
                result.push(node);
            }

            for (let i = 0; i < node.children.length; i++) {
                stack.push(node.children[i]);
            }
        }

        return result;
    }
}
```

This code creates a `GraphManager` class that connects to a Neo4j database and provides methods for adding vertices and edges. It also includes a `DomTraverser` class which uses depth-first search (DFS) algorithm to traverse the DOM tree. 

Please note, this is a basic implementation and does not cover all tradeoffs you mentioned such as handling delays or failures in API calls, optimizing for large number of vertices/edges, etc. You would need to add these features based on your specific requirements. Also, please replace `neo4j-driver` with the actual graph database driver you are using and adjust the code accordingly.
