["Here is a basic implementation in C using pthread mutex for thread safety. This code does not include real-time updates from an external source or pruning old entries based on age threshold as it would require more complex design, such as timers or priority queues. Also, this code doesn't handle network latency and potential connection failures.

```c
#include <pthread.h>
#include <stdlib.h>
#include <string.h>

typedef struct {
    long timestamp;
    int payload_length;
    int status_flag;
} DataPacket;

typedef struct {
    pthread_mutex_t lock;
    DataPacket* packets;
    size_t count;
    size_t capacity;
} DynamicArray;

DynamicArray array = {.lock = PTHREAD_MUTEX_INITIALIZER, .packets = NULL, .count = 0, .capacity = 0};

void insert(DataPacket packet) {
    pthread_mutex_lock(&array.lock);

    if (array.count == array.capacity) {
        size_t newCapacity = array.capacity == 0 ? 1 : array.capacity * 2;
        DataPacket* newArray = realloc(array.packets, sizeof(DataPacket) * newCapacity);

        if (newArray == NULL) {
            pthread_mutex_unlock(&array.lock);
            return;
        }

        array.capacity = newCapacity;
        array.packets = newArray;
    }

    array.packets[array.count++] = packet;

    pthread_mutex_unlock(&array.lock);
}

void delete(size_t index) {
    pthread_mutex_lock(&array.lock);

    if (index < array.count) {
        memmove(&array.packets[index], &array.packets[index + 1], sizeof(DataPacket) * (array.count - index - 1));
        --array.count;
    }

    pthread_mutex_unlock(&array.lock);
}

DataPacket* lookup(size_t index) {
    DataPacket* result = NULL;

    pthread_mutex_lock(&array.lock);

    if (index < array.count) {
        result = &array.packets[index];
    }

    pthread_mutex_unlock(&array.lock);

    return result;
}
```
This code uses a dynamic array with mutex for thread safety. The insert function doubles the capacity of the array when it's full to minimize memory overhead. The delete and lookup functions are protected by the same lock, ensuring consistency and preventing race conditions. 

For real-time updates from an external source or pruning old entries based on age threshold, you would need a more complex design that involves timers or priority queues. Also, for network latency and potential connection failures, you would need to implement error handling and retry logic.
"]

