["Here is an example of how you might implement this in C. This code does not include all the details you requested, but it should give you a good starting point. It's important to note that handling memory allocation failures gracefully without causing memory leaks can be complex and depends on your specific use case. 

```c
#include <stdlib.h>
#include <time.h>

typedef struct NFAPI_CRC_INDICATION_PDU_T {
    int RNTI;
    time_t timestamp;
} NFAPI_CRC_INDICATION_PDU_T;

typedef struct Node {
    NFAPI_CRC_INDICATION_PDU_T data;
    struct Node* next;
} Node;

Node* createNode(int RNTI) {
    Node* newNode = (Node*) malloc(sizeof(Node));
    if (newNode == NULL) {
        // Handle allocation failure gracefully without causing memory leaks.
        return NULL;
    }

    newNode->data.RNTI = RNTI;
    time(&newNode->data.timestamp);
    newNode->next = NULL;

    return newNode;
}

void addNode(Node** head, int RNTI) {
    Node* newNode = createNode(RNTI);
    if (newNode == NULL) {
        // Handle allocation failure gracefully without causing memory leaks.
        return;
    }

    if (*head == NULL) {
        *head = newNode;
    } else {
        Node* current = *head;
        while (current->next != NULL) {
            // Handle collisions by chaining.
            if (current->data.RNTI == RNTI) {
                free(newNode);
                return;
            }

            current = current->next;
        }

        current->next = newNode;
    }
}
```
This code creates a linked list of `NFAPI_CRC_INDICATION_PDU_T` structures. Each node contains an RNTI and a timestamp indicating when the structure was created. The function `createNode` allocates memory for a new node, initializes its data, and sets its next pointer to NULL. If allocation fails, it returns NULL without causing a memory leak.

The function `addNode` adds a new node to the list. It first checks if the list is empty (i.e., the head of the list is NULL). If so, it simply sets the head of the list to be the new node. Otherwise, it traverses the list until it finds either an existing node with the same RNTI or the end of the list. If it finds a node with the same RNTI, it frees the new node and returns without adding it to the list. Otherwise, it adds the new node at the end of the list.
