typedef struct entry {
    char* key;
    int value;
    time_t timestamp;
} Entry;

typedef struct node {
    Entry* data;
    struct node* next;
} Node;

Node* head = NULL;
pthread_mutex_t lock = PTHREAD_MUTEX_INITIALIZER;

Entry* create_entry(char* key, int value) {
    Entry* new_entry = (Entry*)malloc(sizeof(Entry));
    new_entry->key = strdup(key);
    new_entry->value = value;
    new_entry->timestamp = time(NULL);
    return new_entry;
}

void insert_at_beginning(char* key, int value) {
    pthread_mutex_lock(&lock);

    Node* new_node = (Node*)malloc(sizeof(Node));
    new_node->data = create_entry(key, value);
    new_node->next = head;
    head = new_node;

    pthread_mutex_unlock(&lock);
}

void traverse() {
    pthread_mutex_lock(&lock);

    Node* current = head;
    while (current != NULL) {
        // Perform operations here...

        current = current->next;
    }

    pthread_mutex_unlock(&lock);
}
```
This code creates a linked list where each node contains an `Entry` structure. The `insert_at_beginning()` function adds new entries at the beginning of the list, and the `traverse()` function traverses the list to perform operations on each entry. 

The library uses a mutex for synchronization, ensuring that only one thread can access the linked list at a time. This is a simple form of concurrency control and may not be sufficient for high-load scenarios where contention is likely. For more advanced concurrent access handling, you might want to consider using lock-free data structures or other forms of synchronization primitives like semaphores or condition variables.

