']

['Here is an example of how you might implement this in C, using POSIX threads (pthreads) to handle concurrency and dynamic memory allocation for `DataStreamConfig` struct array. This code does not include the integration with external data source or logging system as it would require additional dependencies and specific implementation details that are not provided in the problem statement.

```c
#include <stdio.h>
#include <stdlib.h>
#include <pthread.h>

typedef struct {
    int id;
    long timestamp;
    int active;
} DataStreamConfig;

void* initializeDataStream(void *arg) {
    int streamId = *((int*) arg);
    DataStreamConfig *config = malloc(sizeof(DataStreamConfig));

    if (config == NULL) {
        printf("Memory allocation failed for data stream %d\n", streamId);
        pthread_exit(NULL);
    }

    config->id = streamId;
    config->timestamp = 0L; // initialize with 0 timestamp
    config->active = 1; // set active by default

    printf("Initialized data stream %d\n", streamId);
    pthread_exit(config);
}

void* manageDataStreams() {
    const int MAX_STREAMS = 10000;
    pthread_t threads[MAX_STREAMS];
    DataStreamConfig *streams[MAX_STREAMS];

    for (int i = 0; i < MAX_STREAMS; ++i) {
        int *id = malloc(sizeof(*id));

        if (id == NULL) {
            printf("Memory allocation failed for stream id %d\n", i);
            continue;
        }

        *id = i;
        pthread_create(&threads[i], NULL, initializeDataStream, id);
    }

    for (int i = 0; i < MAX_STREAMS; ++i) {
        void* result;
        pthread_join(threads[i], &result);

        if (result != NULL) {
            streams[i] = (DataStreamConfig*) result;
        } else {
            printf("Failed to initialize data stream %d\n", i);
        }
    }

    // Return the array of initialized streams.
    DataStreamConfig** returnValue = malloc(sizeof(*streams) * MAX_STREAMS);
    if (returnValue == NULL) {
        printf("Memory allocation failed for return value\n");
        pthread_exit(NULL);
    }

    for (int i = 0; i < MAX_STREAMS; ++i) {
        returnValue[i] = streams[i];
    }

    pthread_exit(returnValue);
}
```
This code creates a separate thread for each data stream, initializes it and stores the configuration in dynamic memory. The `manageDataStreams` function returns an array of pointers to these configurations. 

Please note that this is a simplified example and does not include all the requirements mentioned in the problem statement. It also assumes that the external data source for initial configuration settings is available and can be accessed synchronously, which might not always be the case in real-world applications. For more complex scenarios, you would need to use asynchronous programming techniques or consider using a different approach altogether.
']

["Here is a basic implementation in C using pthreads for concurrency control. This code does not include the real-time updates from an external data source or the predefined rules to update event messages as these would require more specific details about those features. 

```c
#include <pthread.h>
#include <stdlib.h>
#include <string.h>
#include <time.h>

typedef struct {
    long timestamp;
    int event_type;
    char* message;
} LogEntry;

typedef struct Node {
    LogEntry log;
    struct Node* next;
} Node;

Node* head = NULL;
pthread_mutex_t lock = PTHREAD_MUTEX_INITIALIZER;

void addLog(long timestamp, int event_type, char* message) {
    pthread_mutex_lock(&lock);

    Node* newNode = (Node*)malloc(sizeof(Node));
    newNode->log.timestamp = timestamp;
    newNode->log.event_type = event_type;
    newNode->log.message = strdup(message);
    newNode->next = head;

    head = newNode;

    pthread_mutex_unlock(&lock);
}

void filterLogsOlderThan(long duration) {
    long threshold = time(NULL) - duration;

    pthread_mutex_lock(&lock);

    Node* prevNode = NULL;
    for (Node* node = head; node != NULL;) {
        if (node->log.timestamp < threshold) {
            if (prevNode == NULL) {
                head = node->next;
            } else {
                prevNode->next = node->next;
            }

            free(node->log.message);
            Node* temp = node;
            node = node->next;
            free(temp);
        } else {
            prevNode = node;
            node = node->next;
        }
    }

    pthread_mutex_unlock(&lock);
}
```
This code uses a linked list to store the logs. Each log entry is stored in a `LogEntry` struct, which contains a timestamp, an event type, and a dynamically allocated message string. The linked list is protected by a mutex lock to ensure thread safety. 

The `addLog()` function adds a new log entry at the head of the list. It allocates memory for the new node and its message string, copies the data into these structures, and then inserts it at the head of the list. The `filterLogsOlderThan()` function removes all logs older than a specified duration by iterating through the list and freeing any nodes that are too old.

This code is simple and efficient for small amounts of data, but could become inefficient with large volumes of data due to its linear time complexity for adding and filtering logs. To handle larger volumes of data, you might want to consider using a more complex data structure or storage solution such as a database. 

In terms of trade-offs between thread safety, performance, and ease of maintenance, this code prioritizes thread safety over performance and ease of maintenance. It uses a mutex lock for all operations on the linked list, which ensures that only one thread can access the list at a time. This makes it safe to use from multiple threads, but could potentially slow down concurrent accesses if there are many threads contending for the lock. 

For handling large volumes of data and managing system dependencies, you might want to consider using a database or other more efficient storage solution. You would also need to carefully manage your system's dependencies to ensure that they can handle larger volumes of data and provide real-time updates from an external data source. This could involve designing your code in such a way that it is modular and easily adaptable, for example by using interfaces or abstract base classes.
