["Here is a basic implementation of such a system in C using pthreads for thread safety. This code does not include all the features you requested (like handling multiple clients concurrently), but it should give you an idea of how to start implementing these features. 

```c
#include <pthread.h>
#include <stdlib.h>
#include <limits.h>

typedef struct {
    int UE_ID;
    float signalStrength;
    float latency;
} NetworkEntityStats;

typedef struct {
    pthread_mutex_t lock;
    NetworkEntityStats *entities;
    int size;
    int capacity;
} EntityManager;

void init(EntityManager *manager) {
    manager->size = 0;
    manager->capacity = 10;
    manager->entities = malloc(manager->capacity * sizeof(NetworkEntityStats));
    pthread_mutex_init(&manager->lock, NULL);
}

void addOrUpdate(EntityManager *manager, NetworkEntityStats entity) {
    pthread_mutex_lock(&manager->lock);

    for (int i = 0; i < manager->size; ++i) {
        if (manager->entities[i].UE_ID == entity.UE_ID) {
            manager->entities[i] = entity;
            pthread_mutex_unlock(&manager->lock);
            return;
        }
    }

    if (manager->size >= manager->capacity) {
        manager->capacity *= 2;
        manager->entities = realloc(manager->entities, manager->capacity * sizeof(NetworkEntityStats));
    }

    manager->entities[manager->size++] = entity;

    pthread_mutex_unlock(&manager->lock);
}

NetworkEntityStats getWeakestSignalStrength(EntityManager *manager) {
    NetworkEntityStats weakest = {0, FLT_MAX, 0};

    pthread_mutex_lock(&manager->lock);

    for (int i = 0; i < manager->size; ++i) {
        if (manager->entities[i].signalStrength < weakest.signalStrength) {
            weakest = manager->entities[i];
        }
    }

    pthread_mutex_unlock(&manager->lock);

    return weakest;
}
```
This code uses a mutex to ensure that only one thread can access the `EntityManager` at a time. This is necessary because multiple threads may be trying to add or update entities concurrently, and we don't want any race conditions. 

The `addOrUpdate` function first checks if an entity with the same UE_ID already exists in the array. If it does, it updates that entity; otherwise, it adds a new one. If the array is full, it doubles its size to prevent frequent reallocations.

The `getWeakestSignalStrength` function simply iterates over all entities and keeps track of the weakest one seen so far. 

This code does not handle multiple clients concurrently or high load scenarios. To handle these, you would need to use a more sophisticated data structure that supports concurrency (like a lock-free hash table) and possibly distribute the work across multiple threads or machines. This is a complex topic beyond the scope of this simple example.
"]

